                    {"type": "mcq", "stem": "Precision, for the matrix in this lecture, is", "choices": ["90/100", "90/120", "160/200", "30/100"], "answer": "B", "why": "Precision is true positives divided by predicted positives, 90/(90+30)."},
                    {"type": "short", "stem": "Why must the test rows be kept out of training?", "answer": "The test is meant to imitate a new case. If the model was fitted on those rows, a high score shows memory, not the ability to handle new data."},
                    {"type": "short", "stem": "A medical screen has recall 0.99 and precision 0.20. What do those two numbers mean in ordinary language?", "answer": "It finds 99 percent of the real cases, and only 20 percent of its alarms are real cases. It rarely misses, and it often alarms when nothing is wrong."},
